# Apache Flink Aggregation Patterns

## Overview
Aggregation is the process of combining multiple events into a single result. In windowed operations, Flink provides several ways to aggregate events efficiently.

## Aggregation Methods

### 1. ProcessWindowFunction

Receives all events in a window as an iterable. Best for complex logic.

```java
DataStream<Result> results = events
    .keyBy(event -> event.getJobId())
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .process(new ProcessWindowFunction<TaskEvent, Result, Integer, TimeWindow>() {
        @Override
        public void process(Integer jobId, Context context,
                          Iterable<TaskEvent> elements,
                          Collector<Result> out) {
            long taskCount = 0;
            for (TaskEvent event : elements) {
                taskCount++;
            }
            out.collect(new Result(jobId, taskCount));
        }
    });
```

**Pros:**
- Full access to window metadata (start time, end time)
- Can emit multiple results per window
- Complete flexibility

**Cons:**
- Holds all events in memory
- Not efficient for large windows

### 2. AggregateFunction

Incrementally combines events. Most efficient.

```java
public class TaskCountAggregator
    implements AggregateFunction<TaskEvent, Long, Long> {

    @Override
    public Long createAccumulator() {
        return 0L;
    }

    @Override
    public Long add(TaskEvent event, Long accumulator) {
        return accumulator + 1L;
    }

    @Override
    public Long getResult(Long accumulator) {
        return accumulator;
    }

    @Override
    public Long merge(Long acc1, Long acc2) {
        return acc1 + acc2;
    }
}

// Usage
DataStream<Long> counts = events
    .keyBy(event -> event.getJobId())
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .aggregate(new TaskCountAggregator());
```

**Pros:**
- Memory efficient (only accumulator stored)
- Fast (incremental updates)
- Parallelizable

**Cons:**
- Limited to single accumulator type
- Less flexible than ProcessWindowFunction

### 3. ReduceFunction

Combines two events at a time. For same input/output type.

```java
public class TaskEventCombiner implements ReduceFunction<TaskEvent> {
    @Override
    public TaskEvent reduce(TaskEvent e1, TaskEvent e2) {
        // Combine two events
        return e1;  // Keep first, discard second for counting
    }
}

DataStream<TaskEvent> combined = events
    .keyBy(event -> event.getJobId())
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .reduce(new TaskEventCombiner());
```

**Pros:**
- Simple for pairwise operations
- Efficient execution

**Cons:**
- Must return same type as input
- Less expressive than aggregate

## Multi-Stage Aggregation

### Pattern: Count Stages, Track Maximum

For finding longest stage per job:

```java
// Step 1: Each session is one stage
// Count tasks in each stage (session)
DataStream<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> stageTaskCounts = events
    .filter(e -> e.eventType == TaskEvent.SUBMIT)
    .keyBy(TaskEvent::getJobId)
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .process(new ProcessWindowFunction<TaskEvent, Tuple2<Integer, Long>, Integer, TimeWindow>() {
        @Override
        public void process(Integer jobId, Context ctx,
                          Iterable<TaskEvent> elements,
                          Collector<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> out) {
            long count = StreamSupport
                .stream(elements.spliterator(), false)
                .count();
            out.collect(new Tuple2<>(jobId, count));
        }
    });

// Step 2: Find maximum per job
DataStream<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> maxPerJob = stageTaskCounts
    .keyBy(t -> t.f0)  // jobId
    .maxBy(1);         // max by task count
```

### Pattern: Multiple Aggregation Functions

```java
public class StageMetadata implements Serializable {
    public long taskCount;
    public long startTime;
    public long endTime;

    public StageMetadata(long count, long start, long end) {
        this.taskCount = count;
        this.startTime = start;
        this.endTime = end;
    }
}

DataStream<Tuple2<Integer, StageMetadata>> stages = events
    .filter(e -> e.eventType == TaskEvent.SUBMIT)
    .keyBy(TaskEvent::getJobId)
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(10)))
    .process(new ProcessWindowFunction<TaskEvent, Tuple2<Integer, StageMetadata>, Integer, TimeWindow>() {
        @Override
        public void process(Integer jobId, Context ctx,
                          Iterable<TaskEvent> elements,
                          Collector<Tuple2<Integer, StageMetadata>> out) {
            List<TaskEvent> eventList = new ArrayList<>();
            elements.forEach(eventList::add);

            long count = eventList.size();
            long startTime = eventList.stream()
                .mapToLong(e -> e.timestamp)
                .min()
                .orElse(0);
            long endTime = eventList.stream()
                .mapToLong(e -> e.timestamp)
                .max()
                .orElse(0);

            out.collect(new Tuple2<>(jobId,
                new StageMetadata(count, startTime, endTime)));
        }
    });
```

## Combining with Job Completion Events

For finding longest stage after job finishes:

```java
// Process job events to know when jobs are finished
DataStream<JobEvent> jobEvents = env
    .readTextFile(jobInput)
    .map(new JobEventParser());

// Filter only job FINISH events
DataStream<JobEvent> finishedJobs = jobEvents
    .filter(e -> e.eventType == JobEvent.FINISH);

// Task stages (from session windows)
DataStream<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> stages = /* as above */;

// Join: keep only stages from finished jobs
DataStream<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> stagesOfFinishedJobs = stages
    .connect(finishedJobs
        .broadcast(new MapStateDescriptor<>("finished_jobs",
            Integer.class, Long.class)))
    .process(new BroadcastProcessFunction<
        Tuple2<Integer, Long>,
        JobEvent,
        Tuple2<Integer, Long>>() {

        private transient MapStateDescriptor<Integer, Long> finishedJobsDescriptor;

        @Override
        public void processElement(Tuple2<Integer, Long> stage,
                                  ReadOnlyContext ctx,
                                  Collector<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> out) {
            // Check if job is finished
