# Filtering Events by Job Completion Status

## Overview
Often we need to process task data only for jobs that have completed (or failed, etc.). This requires correlating task events with job events.

## Job Event Types

From Google cluster-usage dataset:
- 0 = SUBMIT
- 1 = SCHEDULE
- 2 = FINISH
- 3 = FAIL
- 4 = EVICT
- 5 = KILL

For "longest stage after job finishes", we care about jobs with event type 2 (FINISH).

## Simple Approaches

### Approach 1: Load Finished Jobs into State

Read job events first, build a set of finished job IDs in memory:

```java
Set<Integer> finishedJobs = new HashSet<>();

DataStream<JobEvent> jobEvents = env
    .readTextFile(jobInput)
    .map(new JobEventParser());

// First pass: collect finished jobs
jobEvents
    .filter(e -> e.eventType == JobEvent.FINISH)
    .map(JobEvent::getJobId)
    .addSink(new RichSinkFunction<Integer>() {
        @Override
        public void invoke(Integer jobId) {
            finishedJobs.add(jobId);
        }
    });

// Wait for first pass to complete (batch job)
env.execute("CollectFinishedJobs");
```

### Approach 2: Broadcast Finished Jobs

Better for streaming/interactive jobs:

```java
DataStream<JobEvent> jobEvents = env
    .readTextFile(jobInput)
    .map(new JobEventParser());

MapStateDescriptor<Integer, Boolean> finishedJobsDescriptor =
    new MapStateDescriptor<>("finished_jobs",
        Integer.class, Boolean.class);

DataStream<Broadcast<Integer>> finishedJobIds = jobEvents
    .filter(e -> e.eventType == JobEvent.FINISH)
    .map(JobEvent::getJobId)
    .broadcast(finishedJobsDescriptor);

DataStream<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> stageResults = stages
    .connect(finishedJobIds)
    .process(new BroadcastProcessFunction<
        Tuple2<Integer, Long>,  // stage (jobId, taskCount)
        Integer,                 // jobId
        Tuple2<Integer, Long>>() {

        @Override
        public void processElement(Tuple2<Integer, Long> stage,
                                  ReadOnlyContext ctx,
                                  Collector<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> out) {
            Integer jobId = stage.f0;
            ReadOnlyBroadcastState<Integer, Boolean> state =
                ctx.getBroadcastState(finishedJobsDescriptor);

            // Only output if job is finished
            if (state.contains(jobId)) {
                out.collect(stage);
            }
        }

        @Override
        public void processBroadcastElement(Integer jobId,
                                           Context ctx,
                                           Collector<Tuple2<Integer, Long>> out) {
            // Record finished job
            ctx.getBroadcastState(finishedJobsDescriptor)
                .put(jobId, true);
        }
    });
```

### Approach 3: Watermark Synchronization (Advanced)

For truly bounded execution, use watermarks:

```java
// Assign max watermark from job events
DataStream<JobEvent> jobEventsWithWatermarks = jobEvents
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(WatermarkStrategy
        .forMonotonousTimestamps()
        .withTimestampAssigner((event, ts) -> event.timestamp));

// Wait for all job events before processing tasks
DataStream<TaskEvent> taskEventsWithWatermarks = taskEvents
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(WatermarkStrategy
        .forMonotonousTimestamps()
        .withTimestampAssigner((event, ts) -> event.timestamp));
```

## Implementation Pattern for Task Event Filtering

```java
public class JobCompletionFilter {
    // For batch job: collect finished jobs once
    public static Set<Integer> loadFinishedJobs(String jobEventPath)
        throws Exception {
        Set<Integer> finished = new HashSet<>();

        try (Scanner scanner = new Scanner(
            new GZIPInputStream(
                new FileInputStream(jobEventPath)))) {

            while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
                String line = scanner.nextLine();
                String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);

                int eventType = Integer.parseInt(fields[3].trim());
                if (eventType == 2) {  // FINISH
                    int jobId = Integer.parseInt(fields[2].trim());
                    finished.add(jobId);
                }
            }
        }

        return finished;
    }
}
```

Usage in job:

```java
Set<Integer> finishedJobs = JobCompletionFilter.loadFinishedJobs(jobInput);

DataStream<TaskEvent> taskEvents = env
    .readTextFile(taskInput)
    .map(new TaskEventParser())
    .filter(e -> finishedJobs.contains(e.jobId));
```

## Job Event Parser

```java
public class JobEvent implements Serializable {
    public long timestamp;
    public int jobId;
    public int eventType;
    public int priority;
    public int numTasks;

    public JobEvent() {}

    public JobEvent(long timestamp, int jobId, int eventType,
                   int priority, int numTasks) {
        this.timestamp = timestamp;
        this.jobId = jobId;
        this.eventType = eventType;
        this.priority = priority;
        this.numTasks = numTasks;
    }

    public static final int SUBMIT = 0;
    public static final int SCHEDULE = 1;
    public static final int FINISH = 2;
    public static final int FAIL = 3;
    public static final int EVICT = 4;
    public static final int KILL = 5;
}

public class JobEventParser implements MapFunction<String, JobEvent> {
    @Override
    public JobEvent map(String line) throws Exception {
        String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);

        JobEvent event = new JobEvent();
        event.timestamp = Long.parseLong(fields[0].trim());
        event.jobId = Integer.parseInt(fields[2].trim());
        event.eventType = Integer.parseInt(fields[3].trim());
        event.priority = Integer.parseInt(fields[7].trim());
        event.numTasks = Integer.parseInt(fields[6].trim());

        return event;
    }
}
```

## Recommended Approach for This Task

Given the task requirements:
1. Identify stages based on SUBMIT event bursts
2. Find longest stage after job finishes
3. Output results

**Recommended: Approach 3 (Load into memory)**

```java
// In main job
String jobEventsPath = parameters.get("job_input");
Set<Integer> finishedJobs = loadFinishedJobs(jobEventsPath);

DataStream<TaskEvent> taskEvents = env
    .readTextFile(taskInput)
    .map(new TaskEventParser())
    .filter(e -> e.eventType == TaskEvent.SUBMIT &&
                 finishedJobs.contains(e.jobId))
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(WatermarkStrategy
        .forMonotonousTimestamps()
        .withTimestampAssigner((event, ts) -> event.timestamp));
```

Benefits:
- Simple and straightforward
- No complex state management
- Fast filtering with hash lookup
- Works well for batch processing
- No memory issues if finished jobs < 10M

## Error Handling

```java
try {
    Set<Integer> finishedJobs = loadFinishedJobs(jobInput);
    if (finishedJobs.isEmpty()) {
        System.err.println("Warning: No finished jobs found");
    }
} catch (Exception e) {
    throw new RuntimeException("Failed to load job events", e);
}
```

